{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-555923",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To estimate $\\sqrt{72},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $72.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $64={8}^{2}$ and $81={9}^{2}.$\n\nSince $64<72<81,$ we know that the square root of $72$ lies between $8$ and $9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14221205/assets/e931f5e15b35adb850966e40e4ad759c233d9419771998efba5dc91d9ab95bc1.png)\n\nSince $72$ lies between $64$ and $81,$ we expect $\\sqrt{72}$ to lie between $8$ and $9.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8.4}^{2} & =70.56 \\\\ {8.5}^{2} & =72.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $72$ lies between $70.56$ and $72.25,$ so $8.4<\\sqrt{72}<8.5.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $72:$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.4}^{2}$ is $72-70.56=1.44.$\n\n- The distance from $72$ to ${8.5}^{2}$ is $72.25-72=0.25.$\n\nSince $72$ is much closer to $72.25,$ $\\sqrt{72}$ is closer to $8.5.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{72}≈8.5.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
